it's a cold night in Paris and these  young children and their families are  spending it outside most people here are  asylum seekers who say they fled  violence and poverty for a better life  in Europe but few imagined that they'd  end up on the street relying on  charities for survival the six-year-old  girl is from Iran her aunt says they've  been homeless for months we have so many  problems we want to send her to school  but we can't as we don't have a home  these children and their mother who  didn't wish to be identified are from  the Democratic Republic of Congo I don't  know what to do  my children are 5 and 8 years old I want  them to go to school it's so painful for  me to see others go but mine live in the  street Amir is one of a group of  volunteers helping the families he  understands them better than most a  Kurdish refugee he arrived in France two  years ago as a teenager and was homeless  I was very rich my father has a factory  he has a mind my mom was a university  teacher she write two books like when I  come to here is I really shocked if you  come here you're stuck you cannot move  you cannot back in the north of the city  rows of tents and makeshift shelters are  crammed along the ring road campaigners  say that hundreds of children and  teenagers are sleeping on the streets of  Paris often in appalling conditions like  this and what activists say is that the  French government is flouting  international rules by failing to  protect minors under French law all  minors should be protected by the state  but if this charity's de Center staff  say many young people sleep outdoors for  months waiting for a judge to formally  recognize their age there are many  miners that are not recognized as such  so they are not benefiting from help  from the state and basically put back in  the street without any support after  more than a year a judge is finally  ruled that this Afghan teenager ahmed is  a minor now I become  I think good life in France to be there  up there I become good life the French  government says it wants to crack down  on illegal immigration and has tightened  its laws but the authorities tougher  approach seems to deny basic human  rights to some the most vulnerable  Natacha butler al jazeera Paris
 

